her  way to combat misinformation for the  first time the platform will let users  report tweets that they believe are  misleading members will be able to  choose whether the flag tweet pertains  to politics health or other  misinformation the test feature is  currently only available in the us  australia and south korea  twitter notes that this is a small  experiment for now and it may not act on  every flagged tweet  joining us now is kara swisher new york  times contributing opinion writer and  host of the podcast sway it is so good  to see you again and i wonder first of  all what your thoughts are on this ftc  suit whether this one seems more likely  to stick and if it does what's at stake  for facebook it seems like the ftc's  efforts to kind of prod and poke and  regulate facebook over the years  have  not really  drawn a lot of blood they seem to have  made a lot of headlines but i don't know  if they've really had a huge impact  well they haven't done much i mean they  did a fine many many years ago and then  they re-upped it with a consent decree  but not they haven't really done  anything they've actually kind of passed  and given them a parking ticket for the  most part this is their most aggressive  effort so far and i think it's going to  be difficult for them because of judges  and there needs to be a rewrite of  anti-trust laws to be able to deal with  companies like facebook and amazon and  many others that don't fit into the  traditional way antitrust is viewed  today by judges and i think that's going  to be their biggest challenge is not  necessarily people thinking it's a good  idea or other things but either congress  has to act and change anti-trust laws  and rewrite them which it's working on  in some ways or judges have to be  amenable to their arguments which is  very difficult it's a it's a pretty high  bar that the fdc is going to have to  climb now that it has aggressive people  in place willing to do these kind of  things  where do you 